counterfactuals.output:
The study analyzes how space-time symmetries are represented in interacting scalar quantum field theory on static curved space-times, using a Euclidean/Riemannian construction as a bridge to the Lorentzian theory.

It begins with scalar fields on static space-times, where the metric admits a preferred time direction and the dynamics can be related to an elliptic operator on the associated spatial manifold. Instead of constructing the Lorentzian quantum theory directly, the approach uses a Euclidean formulation on a Riemannian manifold. In this setting, the field theory is described by probability measures or Schwinger functions associated with an interacting scalar field. Reflection positivity, covariance, and suitable analytic properties allow one to reconstruct a Hilbert space, a Hamiltonian, and a unitary time evolution.

A central technical object is a self-adjoint semigroup generated by the Euclidean Hamiltonian. The semigroup encodes imaginary-time evolution and is used to prove the existence of a physical Hilbert space and a positive self-adjoint Hamiltonian after Osterwalder–Schrader-type reconstruction. Staticity makes this particularly natural: Euclidean time translations become a contraction semigroup, whose generator becomes the Lorentzian Hamiltonian after analytic continuation.

The work then studies how symmetries of the underlying space-time act on the quantum theory. Isometries of the Euclidean/Riemannian manifold that preserve the relevant structure act on the Euclidean fields and measures. Under appropriate invariance and positivity assumptions, these transformations descend to the reconstructed Hilbert space as unitary operators. Thus, classical geometric symmetries become quantum symmetries. In particular, one obtains a unitary representation of the space-time symmetry group, or of a subgroup compatible with the chosen static decomposition and reflection structure.

For interacting scalar fields, the main point is that the Euclidean construction still permits rigorous control of the symmetry action. The interaction is introduced through a local potential, often of polynomial type, and the resulting measure or functional integral is shown to retain the necessary covariance properties. This allows the interacting theory, not just the free theory, to inherit the geometric symmetries.

A key example discussed is hyperbolic space and its relation to Anti-de Sitter space. Euclidean Anti-de Sitter space can be represented by hyperbolic space, while Lorentzian Anti-de Sitter space arises by analytic continuation. Hyperbolic space has a large isometry group, and the study shows how these isometries are implemented in the reconstructed quantum theory. In the AdS case, the Euclidean symmetry group corresponds to the Lorentzian AdS symmetry group after continuation, leading to a unitary representation of the AdS isometry group on the interacting quantum Hilbert space.

The hyperbolic/Anti-de Sitter example is important because it illustrates the general mechanism in a maximally symmetric curved background. The Euclidean theory on hyperbolic space provides a mathematically controlled construction of an interacting scalar field, while the reconstruction procedure yields a Lorentzian AdS quantum field theory with unitary time evolution and symmetry implementation.

In summary, the study shows that:

- interacting scalar QFTs on static space-times can be constructed using Euclidean Riemannian methods;
- self-adjoint semigroups encode imaginary-time evolution and lead to positive Hamiltonians;
- reflection positivity enables the passage from Euclidean fields to Lorentzian Hilbert-space quantum theory;
- geometric isometries of the Euclidean manifold become unitary symmetries after quantization;
- in the hyperbolic space/Anti-de Sitter example, the construction yields a unitary interacting scalar QFT carrying the expected AdS space-time symmetries.
